 (XLS)\nL598: ### cite270†Table S3. L599: \nL600: Comparison of GIs to manually curated gene pairs compiled from low-throughput experimental studies.\nL601: \nL602: cite271†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1004120.s024†doi.org L603: \nL604: (XLS)\nL605: \nL606: ### cite272†Table S4. L607: \nL608: Monochromatic GIs that are significant (sheet 1) and non-significant (sheet 2) between bacterial processes.\nL609: \nL610: cite273†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1004120.s025†doi.org L611: \nL612: (XLS)\nL613: ### cite274†Table S5. L614: \nL615: Conservation of YaiF among microbes.\nL616: \nL617: cite275†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1004120.s026†doi.org L618: \nL619: (XLS)\nL620: \nL621: ### cite276†Table S6. L622: \nL623: Average number of GIs identified per donor query chaperone.\nL624: \nL625: cite277†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1004120.s027†doi.org L626: \nL627: (XLS)\nL628: \nL629: ### cite278†Table S7. L630: \nL631: Number of epistatic interactions between chaperone to chaperone and chaperone to non-chaperone.\nL632: \nL633: cite279†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1004120.s028†doi.org L634: \nL635: (XLS)\nL636: ### cite280†Table S8. L637: \nL638: Epistatic interaction of non-chaperone gene to chaperones targeted in the study.\nL639: \nL640: cite281†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1004120.s029†doi.org L641: \nL642: (XLS)\nL643: \nL644: ### cite282†Table S9. L645: \nL646: Correlation coefficient scores for each gene across all other genes in the E. coli genome.\nL647: \nL648: cite283†https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pgen.1004120.s030†doi.org L649: \nL650: (XLSX)\nL651: ### cite284†Table S10. L652: \nL653: Number of GIs of each chaperone mutant with one or more chaperone-containing protein complexes (sheet 1-I). Presence or absence of epistatic association of a chaperone gene deletion mutant with one or more chaperone-containing protein complexes (sheet 1-II). Overlap of GIs among the chaper